                title: "NP Problems and Complexity Classes",

                overview:
                "Computational Complexity Theory is a branch of computer science that classifies problems based on the amount of computational resources required to solve them. Complexity classes such as P, NP, NP-Complete, and NP-Hard help determine whether a problem can be solved efficiently or whether only its solution can be verified efficiently. These concepts are fundamental in algorithm design, optimization, artificial intelligence, cryptography, scheduling, and theoretical computer science, and are among the most frequently asked topics in technical interviews and competitive examinations.",

                definition:
                "Complexity Classes are categories of computational problems classified according to the time required to solve or verify them. P problems are efficiently solvable, NP problems are efficiently verifiable, NP-Complete problems are both NP and NP-Hard, while NP-Hard problems are at least as difficult as NP-Complete problems.",

