                interviewQuestions: [

                    {
                        question: "What is Computational Complexity?",

                        answer:
                        "Computational Complexity is the study of the resources required by an algorithm to solve a problem as the input size increases. It mainly analyzes time complexity and space complexity and helps classify problems based on their computational difficulty."
                    },

                    {
                        question: "What is a P Problem?",

                        answer:
                        "A P Problem is a decision problem that can be solved by a deterministic algorithm in polynomial time. Examples include graph connectivity, shortest path with suitable assumptions, and minimum spanning tree decision variants."
                    },

                    {
                        question: "What is an NP Problem?",

                        answer:
                        "An NP Problem is a decision problem for which a proposed solution can be verified in polynomial time by a deterministic algorithm. NP stands for Nondeterministic Polynomial time."
                    },

                    {
                        question: "Differentiate P and NP.",

                        answer:
                        "P contains decision problems that can be solved in polynomial time by a deterministic algorithm. NP contains decision problems whose proposed solutions can be verified in polynomial time. Every P problem is also in NP, so P is a subset of NP. Whether P equals NP is an open problem."
                    },

                    {
                        question: "What is an NP-Complete Problem?",

                        answer:
                        "An NP-Complete problem is a decision problem that belongs to NP and is also NP-Hard. This means its solution can be verified in polynomial time, and every problem in NP can be reduced to it in polynomial time."
                    },

                    {
                        question: "What is an NP-Hard Problem?",

                        answer:
                        "An NP-Hard problem is a problem that is at least as hard as every problem in NP, meaning every NP problem can be reduced to it in polynomial time. An NP-Hard problem does not necessarily have to be a decision problem or belong to NP."
                    },

                    {
                        question: "Differentiate NP-Complete and NP-Hard.",

                        answer:
                        "An NP-Complete problem must be both in NP and NP-Hard, meaning its solutions can be verified in polynomial time and it is at least as hard as every problem in NP. An NP-Hard problem only needs to satisfy the hardness condition and may not belong to NP."
                    },

                    {
                        question: "Explain the relationship between P, NP, NP-Complete, and NP-Hard.",

                        answer:
                        "P is a subset of NP because every problem that can be solved in polynomial time can also be verified in polynomial time. NP-Complete problems are the problems that are both in NP and NP-Hard. NP-Hard problems include all NP-Complete problems and may also include problems outside NP. It is currently unknown whether P equals NP."
                    },

                    {
                        question: "What is the Travelling Salesman Problem?",

                        answer:
                        "The Travelling Salesman Problem, or TSP, asks for the minimum-cost tour that visits every given city exactly once and returns to the starting city. Its decision version is NP-Complete, while the optimization version is NP-Hard."
                    },

                    {
                        question: "Why are NP-Complete problems important?",

                        answer:
                        "NP-Complete problems are important because they represent some of the hardest problems in NP. If any NP-Complete problem is solved by a polynomial-time algorithm, then every problem in NP can also be solved in polynomial time, which would imply P = NP."
                    }

                ],

